What do you think? Imagine someone who has one hundred sheep, and one of them goes missing. Won't he leave the ninety-nine sheep in the wilderness and go search for the lost sheep?
What I am going to tell you now is true. If he finds this sheep, he will be happier with it than with 99 sheep that were not lost.
This is the will of your Father who is in heaven. For he does not want to lose any of these little ones. When someone sins against you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. And keep it between the two of you. If he listens to you, you will have him back again.
But what if he does not listen to you? Take one or two other people with you. The Bible says, "At the mouth of two or three witnesses every word will be established" (Deuteronomy 19:15).
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then report that to the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then do not treat him as your brother. Treat him as you would treat someone who is not a believer or a tax collector.
What I am going to tell you now is true. What you shut up on earth will be shut up in heaven. And what you open on earth will be open in heaven.
Again, this is what I tell you. If two of you agree on earth about anything they ask for, it will be done for them by my Father who is in heaven.
"Where two or three are gathered in my name, I am there among them." The slave who had no mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and said to him, "Lord, how many times shall I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?" . الشرح: The task was a direct translation. The user provided the original text in a mix of Modern Standard Arabic and potentially Levantine Arabic elements (specifically the use of "خويّا" for "my brother"). The goal was to translate this into English, maintaining the core meaning and intent of the original text, while also considering the nuances of the Arabic terms used.
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The parable of the Kingdom of Heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his slaves for everything they owed him.
And when the king began to do that, they brought him a man who owed him millions of dollars.
The man was not able to pay. Therefore, his master gave an order. The man, his wife, his children, and all that he possessed were to be sold to pay what he owed.
The server fell to his knees before him. And he begged him, saying, Give me some time, I will return everything to you.
His master, having compassion on him, released him from the debt he owed him, and let him go.
But that servant went out later on and found one of the other servants who owed him a few dollars. So he grabbed him and began to strangle him. And he said to him, 'Pay back what you owe me!'
And the other servant fell on his knees. He begged, "Give me time, and I will return the money to you."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and had the man thrown into prison. He would be held there until he could repay what he owed.
He saw the other servants who fell. This made them very scared. They went and told their master everything that happened.
Then the master summoned the first servant and said to him, "You wicked servant! I forgave you all the debt you owed me because you pleaded with me.
Did it not occur to you that you should have compassion for the other servant, just as I have compassion for you?
His master being angry with him, delivered him to the gaolers, who were going to punish him, until he should pay all that he owed.
This is how my Father in heaven will treat each of you, unless you forgive your brother or sister from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
And what Jesus did not complete, this is the reason for the talk. He left the regions of Galilee and went to the Jewish regions that exist on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. And he healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him, and they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read that at the beginning the Creator 'made them male and female'?" (Genesis 1:27).
For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be united to his wife, and they will become one flesh. (Genesis 2:24)
What God has put together, let no man tear asunder.
They asked: "So why did Moses command that a man could give his wife a divorce document and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because of the hardness of your hearts, but it was not like that from the beginning."
This is what I am telling you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man should not divorce his wife unless she is not faithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If this is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone is able to accept the idea of remaining single. Only those who have been helped to live without marrying can accept that.
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. Others have been made that way by other people. And others have made themselves that way in order to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. And whoever can accept living in this way, they should do it." The little children were brought to Jesus.
Some people brought children to Jesus. They wanted him to place his hands on them and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said: "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
And Jesus placed his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to obtain eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter the kingdom, you must obey the commandments."
Which? asked the man. And Jesus said: Thou shalt not kill. Thou shalt not commit adultery. Thou shalt not steal. Thou shalt not bear false witness.
Honor your father and your mother. Love your neighbor as yourself.
The young man said, "I have obeyed all these commandments. What must I do next?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When this young man heard this talk, he went away grieved. He was very rich.
And after that, Jesus said to his disciples, "This I tell you now, is true. It is hard for the rich to enter the Kingdom of Heaven.
And I say to you again: It is hard for a camel to go through the eye of a needle. But it is easier for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God."
And when the students heard this thing, they were very surprised. And they asked him: "Is there anyone who can afford to be dismissed?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "This is impossible for mortals, but all things are possible for God."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything and followed you! What then will we have?"
Jesus said to them, "What I tell you is the truth. When all things are renewed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. And then, you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones. You will judge the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left a home, or family, or fields for my sake, will receive a hundred times more. And they will inherit eternal life.
But many of the first shall be last, and many of the last shall be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The parable of the Kingdom of Heaven is like a man who owned a piece of land. He went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
He went out again around 9 a.m. He saw others standing in the market doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard, and I will give you what is fair."
So they went. "Then he went out another time around noon, and in the third time after noon, and he did the same thing.
He went out at about five o'clock and found others still standing around him. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day long without doing anything?"
They answered, "Because no one has hired us." He said to them, "Go also you and do work in my vineyard."
In the evening, he spoke with the vineyard owners, along with the man who was in charge of the workers. He told him: Gather the workers and give them their wages. He started with the last ones you served, and then he gave them to the first ones.
The workers you hired arrived around five o'clock. Each one received the pay for a normal day.
And so, when those who had been hired first came, they expected to receive more. But each of them also received the usual day's wage.
When they took possession of it, they started complaining about its owner.
They said: "These recently hired people worked for just one hour. You paid them the same amount that you paid us. We did most of the work and were under the hot sun all day."
The owner answered one of them, "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the usual daily wage?"
Take your money and leave. I want to give the same salary I gave you to the people I recently worked with.
Don't I have the right to do what I want with my money? Do you feel cheated because I've given so much to other people?
Therefore, those who are last will be first, and those who are first will be last." Jesus spoke again about his coming death.
Jesus was on his way to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
"We will go to Jerusalem," he said. "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jews. People will mock him and whip him. They will nail him to a cross. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead!" Or she asks Jesus for a favor
The woman who is the mother of the sons of Zebidi is with Jesus. Her children came with her. She knelt down and asked him for something.
What is it that you want? asked Jesus. And she said: He promised me that my two sons would sit, one at your right hand and one at your left, in your kingdom.
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I am to drink?" They said to Him, "We can."
Jesus said to them, "You will certainly drink from my cup, but it is not for me to say who will sit on my right or left, for those places are reserved for those for whom my Father has prepared them."
The other ten pupils heard about it. They were angry at the two brothers.
Jesus gathered them and said to them: "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them.
Don't be like that. And the one among you who wants to be important must be your servant.
And everyone who wants to be first must be your servant.
Become like a human child. He did not come to be served, but to serve people. He gave his life to sacrifice it and free many people from slavery. He returned two blind men who could see.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed him.
There were two blind men sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, "Lord, have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. They asked them to be silent. But the two men cried out even louder: "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stood up and called out to them. And he asked them, "What do you want me to do for you?"
They answered, "Lord, we wish that we could see."
Jesus was deeply concerned for them. He touched their eyes, and they could see immediately. They followed him.
Jesus' Entry into Jerusalem
And when they drew near, all together, to Jerusalem, and they came to the Pool of Bethesda, which was in Solomon's Porch. Then Jesus sent two disciples,
He told them, "Go to the village before you. As soon as you reach it, you will find a donkey tied up. Its foal will be with it. Untie them and bring them to me."
If someone tells you something, tell them the Lord needs them. Their owner will send them to you right away.
This happened so that the words spoken by the prophet might be fulfilled. For it says:
"Tell the Daughter of Zion, 'Behold, your king is coming to you, gentle and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey.'" (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had told them to do.
They brought the donkey and its baby. They put their djellabas on them. And then Jesus sat on top of the djellabas.
A very large crowd spread out and placed their coats on the road. Others cut branches from trees and spread them on the road.
Some people walked in front of him, and others followed. And they all cried out: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The multitude answered: "This is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple. And he began to drive out all those who were buying and selling there. And he overturned the tables of the moneychangers. As he also overturned the seats of those who sold doves.
He said to them: 'It is written: The Lord has said: My house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples. (Isaiah 56:7) But you have made it a den of robbers. (Jeremiah 7:11)'
They came to Jesus in the synagogue of those who were blind and those who had disabilities. And he drove their demons out of them.
And the chief priests and the scribes saw the miracles that he did. And they also saw the children who were crying out in the temple: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" But when they saw all this, they were filled with rage.
Do you hear what these young people are saying? they asked. Jesus replied: Yes. Have you never read in the Scriptures: 'You have made them a little lower than the angels; you have crowned them with glory and honor'? (Psalm 8: 5-6).
Then Jesus left the crowd and went out of the city to Bethany, and there he passed the night. A fig tree's branches dried up.
Jesus was on his way back to Jerusalem early in the morning. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the roadside. He walked over to it, but found nothing on it but leaves. And he said to it: "No more shall you be fruitful for any purpose whatever." And immediately the tree withered.
And when my students saw this thing, they were amazed. And they said: How is it that the fig tree dried up with such speed?
Jesus answered, "What I tell you is truth. You must have faith and not doubt. Then you can do what was done to the fig tree. And you can say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea.' It will be done."
"If you believe, you will receive whatever you ask for when you pray." Questioning Jesus' authority.
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. "By what authority are you doing these things?" they asked. "Who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "I will also ask you one question. If you tell me the answer, then I will tell you by what authority I am doing these things.
From where did the baptism of John come? Was it from heaven, or from men?" They disputed with one another, reasoning, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Then why did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say: "Of the men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone thinks that John was a prophet.
They answered Jesus, "We do not know." Jesus said to them, "I also will not tell you by what authority I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said to him: My son, go and work today in the vineyard.
"I will not do that," the son answered. But he later changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son replied: "I will, sir," but he did not go.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" And they said, "The first." Then Jesus said to them, "Truly, I say to you, that tax collectors and prostitutes enter into the kingdom of God before you."
John came to show you the right way to live. And you did not believe in him. But tax collectors and prostitutes believed in him. You saw this. But even after that, you did not repent of your sins and believe in him. The parable of the tenants.
Listen to another story. There was a man who had a piece of land, so he planted a vineyard in it, and he surrounded it with a fence, and he dug a winepress in it, and he built a watchtower in it. Then he rented the vineyard to some farmers, and he traveled far away on a journey.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. He instructed the servants to collect his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his servants. And they beat one of them. And they killed another. And they stoned the third to death.
And after that, the man sent more servants to the tenants. He sent more than the first time. The tenants treated them in the same way.
Finally, he sent his son to them. And he said: They will respect my son.
But the tenants saw the son coming. And they said to each other, "This is the one who will inherit all the owner's property someday. Let's kill him, and then all the property will be ours."
And they seized him and brought him out of the vineyard and killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do to these tenants?
"He will destroy those wicked ones," they answered. "Then he will rent the vineyard to other tenants. And they will give him his share of the crop at harvest time."
Jesus said to them: "Have you not read in the Scriptures, 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone'? This is from the Scriptures."
So, this is what I say to you: The kingdom of God will be taken from you and given to a people that will produce its fruits.
Everyone who falls on that rock will be broken into pieces. But the rock will crush anyone it falls on.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard the stories about Jesus. They knew that he was talking about them.
They sought a way to arrest him, but they feared the crowds. For the people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
And Jesus continued to tell them more stories. He said:
