Then he sent some other servants. He said, 'Tell those invited, I have prepared my dinner. I have killed my fattened oxen. All is ready. Come to the wedding supper.'
But the people paid no attention. One went to his field, another to his work.
The rest of them seized his servants, treated them badly, and then killed them.
The king was filled with rage. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
And afterward, the king said to his servants: 'The wedding is ready, but the people I invited were not worthy to attend.'
Go to the alley of the street. Call for dinner for everyone you find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, whether they were good or evil. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. There he noticed a man who was not dressed in the wedding clothes.
"My friend," he asked, "how did you get in here without the wedding clothes?" The man couldn't think of anything to say.
And afterward, the king said to his servants: "Tie his hands and feet and take him outside into the darkness. There, people will weep and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and immediately plotted how they might entangle Jesus in what He said.
They sent their disciples to him, and they sent along the Herodians. And they said to him, "Teacher, we know that you are a truthful man, and you do not care about anyone, for you do not look at people's status."
So tell us, what do you think? Is it permissible to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus was aware of their evil schemes, and he said, "Hypocrites! Why are you trying to trap me?"
"Show me the coin used by people to pay tax." They showed him a silver coin.
He asked them: "Whose is this picture? And whose are these words?"
"Caesar," they said. Then he said to them, "Give to Caesar what is Caesar's, and to God what is God's."
When they heard this, they were struck dumb. So they left him and went away. Marriage when the dead rise
On that day, the Sadducees came to Jesus and asked him a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
"Teacher," they said, "this is what Moses said to us. If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name.
There were seven brothers among us. The first got married and then died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second and third brothers. This continued until the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
And so, when the dead rise, to whom will the wife belong? They were all seven married to her."
Jesus answered, "You are wrong, because you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God."
When the dead come back to life, they do not get married. And their parents do not give them anything to get married with. They will be like the angels in heaven.
"Is there no resurrection of the dead? Have you not read what God said to you?"
He said, "I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob" (Exodus 3:6). "He is not the God of the dead, but the God of the living."
And when the crowds heard this, they were astonished at his teachings. The most important commandment.
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees could not answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them, an expert in the law, tested Jesus with a question.
He said to him, "Teacher, what is the most important commandment in the law?"
Jesus answered: "You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind" (Deuteronomy 6:5).
This is the first and most important commandment.
And the second is like it: "Love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18).
All that is written in the Law and the Prophets hangs on these two commandments." To whom is Christ son?
The Pharisees met together. And Jesus asked them,
"What do you all believe about the Messiah? Who was he born to?" "He was born to David," they answered them.
And he said to them, "How then does David in spirit call him Lord, saying,
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool."
If David calls him Lord, how can he be David's son?"
No one could answer him even with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him a question.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said, "The scribes and Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
So you must obey them. Do everything they ask of you. But don't do what they do. They don't practice what they preach.
They tie heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of other people. But they themselves do not want to move even a finger to carry it.
All they do is for the sake of others' seeing it. They put on their foreheads and arms small boxes containing verses from the Bible. They make these boxes very broad. And they make the tassels on their coats very long.
They reserve and sit in the places of honor above the dining table. They reserve all the most important places in the mosque.
They want to be greeted in the markets. They want it when people call them "rabbi".
But do not call anyone on earth 'Rabbi,' for you have one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth your father, for you have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You shall not be called 'Teacher'. You have only one Teacher, the Christ.
The most important person among you will be your servant.
Whoever exalts himself will be humbled; and whoever is humbled will be exalted.
Oh, the horror of what will happen to you, you teachers of the law and Pharisees! You hypocrites! You have shut the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces. For neither will you enter, nor will you allow those who are entering to go in.
Translate
Woe to you, you teachers of the law and you Pharisees, you hypocrites! You cross sea and land to make a single convert, and when he becomes one, you make him twice as much a child of hell as yourselves.
Oh you, what a pair of blind guides! You say, "If someone swears by the Temple, his oath is invalid. But if someone swears by the gold in the Temple, he is bound by his oath."
You are blind and foolish! What is the most important thing? Is it gold? Or the temple that makes gold sacred?
"For even they say, 'If anyone swears by the altar, it is nothing; but if anyone swears by the gift that is on the altar, he must be held to his oath.'
O ye blind! which is more important? the gift? or the altar which makes the gift sacred?
Therefore, anyone who swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And anyone who swears by the temple, he swears by it and by him who dwells in it.
And anyone who swears by heaven, swears by the throne of God and by the one who sits upon it.
Oh you, the teachers of Sharia, and the Pharisees, the hypocrites! How severe your punishment will be! For you neglect the most important thing in the Sharia, which is justice, mercy, and honesty. You should have worked by these things without neglecting the other.
O blind guides, who strain out a gnat and swallow a camel whole!
O you, what a group you are, you teachers of Sharia and Farisiyya, you hypocritical Pharisees! How severe your destruction is! For you clean the outside of the cup and the dish, but inside them both is full of extortion and excess.
You blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and the dish, so that the outside of them may also be clean.
Oh, the terrible things you are doing, you teachers of the law and you Pharisees, you hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but on the inside are full of the bones of the dead and of all kinds of uncleanliness.
This applies to you as well. From the outside, it looks like you are doing what is right. But on the inside, you are full of what is wrong. You pretend that you are not what you are.
Oh, what a group of my teachers of Sharia and the Farisiyya hypocrites! How severe is your destruction! For you are building graves for the prophets and decorating the tombs of the righteous.
And you would have said, 'If we had been with those who were before us, we would not have done what they did.' And We would not have been to destroy the prophets with falsehood.
So you are testifying against yourselves, and you admit that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
So complete the sins that those who lived before you began!
O nest of deadly vipers! How will you escape being condemned to hell?
So I am sending you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, and you will crucify some of them, and some of them you will flog in your temples. And you will pursue them from city to city.
Therefore, you will pay for all the blood of the righteous that has been shed on earth. I mean from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Berekiah. Zechariah is the one you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I am going to tell you now is true. All of this is going to happen to the people who are alive now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you. I have often wanted to gather your people together, as a hen gathers her brood under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, your house is empty, it's become empty.
I say to you: You will not see me from now on until you say: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord. (Psalm 118:26)
End of the world signs
Jesus left the temple. As he was walking away, his disciples approached him. They wanted to draw his attention to the temple buildings.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "Truly I tell you, not one stone here will be left on another; every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. And there came to him the disciples privately. And they said to him, "Tell us, when will these things be? And what is the sign of your coming? And what is the sign of the end of the age?"
Jesus answered, "Beware! Be vigilant, lest someone deceive you.
Many people will come in my name. They will say, "I am the Messiah!" and they will deceive many people.
You will hear about wars. You will hear people talking about the wars that will be in the future. Do not be afraid. This has to happen. But the end is not yet here.
Nation will rise against nation. Kingdom will rise against kingdom. There will be famines. There will be earthquakes in many places.
All of this is the beginning of labor pains.
"Then the people will deliver you to be treated badly and killed. And all the nations will hate you for my sake.
At that time, many people will renounce their religion. They will hate each other. Some of them will betray each other to their enemies.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because lawlessness will increase, the love of most will grow cold.
But the one who endures to the end, that one will be saved.
This good news of the kingdom will be preached in the whole world for a witness to all the nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the detestable thing that causes desolation." (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) For at a future time, you will see standing in the holy place, the one who causes desolation. As for this matter, the reader must understand it.
And after that, those who are in Judaism must flee to the mountains.
There is nobody on the roof, he needs to come down to his house to take something with him.
No one in this field should have to come back to get their cap.
How arduous that day will be for pregnant women! And how arduous it will be for nursing mothers!
Pray so you don't need to escape in the winter or on a Saturday.
Those days will be extremely intense suffering, a lot. It will be more evil than everything that has happened since the world was created until now. And it will not be another time something like this.
If the time is not shortened, no one will survive. But because of God's chosen people, it will be shortened.
Here is the translation: "Someone will say to you at that time: 'Look, here is the Messiah!' Or: 'Here he is, over there!' Do not believe it."
False messiahs and false prophets will rise and produce signs and wonders to lead astray, if possible, God's elect.
Look, I told you this before.
"If someone says to you, 'Look, here is the Messiah, in the desert,' do not go out. And if someone says, 'Look, here he is, in the inner rooms,' do not believe it."
Lightning comes from the east and is visible even in the west. This will also be the case when the Son of Man comes.
Vultures will gather wherever there is a dead carcass.
Immediately after the terrible sufferings they endured during those days, "the sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, and the stars of heaven shall fall, and the heavens shall depart as a scroll that is rolled up." (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
At that time, the sign of the Son of Man will appear in the sky. And all the nations of the earth will mourn. And they will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of the sky. And He will come with power and great glory.
He will send His angels with a loud trumpet call. He will gather His chosen people from the four corners of the earth. He will bring them from one end of the heavens to the other.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. As soon as its branches become tender and it puts forth leaves, you know that summer is near.
In the same way, when you see all these things happening, you know that the end is near. It is at the door.
What I am about to tell you now is true. The people who were living at that time would not die until all these events took place.
The heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away. No one knows the day or the hour.
No one knows about that day or hour, not even the angels in heaven. The Son does not know, only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days that preceded the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all these things until the day that Noah entered the ark.
They did not know anything about what was going to happen until the floods came and swept them all away. This is what is going to be like when the Son of Man comes.
There will be two men in the field. One will be taken and the other will be left.
There will be two women grinding with hand mills. They will take one and leave the other.
Be alert, you do not know on what day your Lord will come.
You need to understand something. Let's assume that the homeowner knew exactly what time of night the thief would come. In that case, he would have stayed up. And he would not have let his home be burglarized.
Therefore, you too must be ready; for the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect.
Let us suppose that a master has appointed one of his servants to be responsible for the other servants in his house. The servant's task is to give them their food at the proper time. The master wants a servant who is faithful and wise for this purpose.
If the master returns and finds his servant doing his work, this will be a good thing for the servant.
What I am going to tell you now is true. The master is going to give this servant responsibility over everything he has.
But let us suppose that the servant is wicked. And let us suppose that he said to himself: 'My master is going to stay away a long time.'
Suppose he began to ill-treat the other servants, and suppose that he ate and drank with those who drank heavily.
This servant will return on a day that the servant does not expect. He will return at a time that the servant does not know.
And after that, they will cut the gentleman into pieces. And they will send him to the place where the liars go. And there, the people will weep and grind their teeth.
The Tale of Ten Maidens
Here is how the kingdom of heaven will be at that time. Ten bridesmaids took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were fools. Five of them were wise.
The fools took their lanterns with them, but they didn't take their olive oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in jars with their lamps.
The groom didn't come for a long time. That's why the women helping the bride got tired and fell asleep.
In the middle of the night, someone shouted: Here is the groom! Come out to meet him!
Then the bride's maidens all awoke and prepared their lamps.
The foolish said to the wise, "Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are about to go out."
"No," they answered. "There may not be enough for all of us. Go instead to those who are selling oil. Buy some for yourselves."
So they went to buy oil. But on their way, the groom arrived. And the women who were ready went in with him for the wedding dinner. And the door was closed.
After that, the other bridesmaids also came. And they said: "Sir! Sir! Open the door for us."
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you is true. I do not know you."
Therefore, be alert, because you do not know either the day or the hour when the bridegroom will come. The story of three servants.
Again, this is what the kingdom of heaven will be like. There was a man traveling. He called his servants and entrusted his property to them.
He gave 10,000 dollars to one. And he gave 4,000 dollars to another. And he gave 2,000 dollars to the third. The man gave each servant the amount he knew the servant could take care of. Then he continued his journey.
The server who received $10,000 immediately went and invested his money. He earned an additional $10,000.
The one who took 4000 dollars, took 4000 extra dollars.
But the man who received two thousand dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. He hid in it and hid his master's money.
After a long time, the master of those servants returned. He wanted to collect all the money they had earned.
The man who took 10,000 dollars brought another 10,000 dollars. He said: "Sir, you trusted me and gave me 10,000 dollars. Look, I won another 10,000 dollars."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things, so I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
Sir, you trusted me with $4000. Look, I have earned another $4000.
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things, so I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
Then the man who had received the two thousand dollars came. And he said, Master, I knew that you were a hard man, reaping where you did not sow and gathering where you did not scatter.
And that's why you were afraid. You went out and buried your $2000 in the ground. Look, this is what comes back to you.'
His master answered and said unto him, O wicked and slothful servant! For didst thou not know that I reap where I sow not? And do I gather where I have not scattered?
So, you should have put my money in the bank. And when I came back, I would have gotten it back with interest.
Then his master said to the other servants, 'Take his pound of flesh and give it to the one who has ten thousand.'
Everyone who has something will get more. They will have more than they need. And what about those who have nothing? Even what they have will be taken away from them.
They kicked that good-for-nothing servant out into the darkness. There in the darkness, people will weep and gnash their teeth.' The sheep and the goats.
Giggi, the son of man, is born in all his glory. And all the angels will come with him. And after that, he will sit on his throne in the glory of heaven.
All nations will gather before him. And he will separate people one from another, as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats.
He will place the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.
"Then the King will speak to those on His right, saying, 'Blessed by My Father. Come, inherit the Kingdom prepared for you since the creation of the world.'"
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you took me in.
I needed clothes and you gave them to me. I was sick and you took care of me. I was in prison and you came to visit me.
Then the righteous who did good deeds will answer him, saying: "Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you? And when did we see you thirsty and give you a drink?"
When did we see you as a stranger and welcome you? When did we see you in need of things and give them to you?
"When have we ever seen you sick, in jail, and we went to visit you?"
The king will answer, 'What I tell you is true. Whatever you did for one of these least brothers of mine, you did for me.'"
"Then He will say to those on His left, 'Depart from Me, you cursed, into the everlasting fire which was prepared for the devil and his angels.'"
I was hungry. But you didn't give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you didn't give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger, and you did not receive me. I was naked and you did not give me anything. I was sick and in prison, and you did not take care of me.
And they will also answer: 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty or a stranger or in need of clothes or sick or in prison, and we did not help you?'"
Ghayguel: "This is exactly what I'm going to tell you. It's the truth. Everything I didn't do for someone among these people who have no value, I didn't do for myself either."
Then they go away to receive punishment forever. As for those who did good deeds, they will receive eternal life.
The plan to kill Jesus.
And when He had finished speaking, Jesus said to His disciples:
As you know, Easter is two days away. And the Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified on a cross.
And afterward the leading priests and the elders of the people assembled in the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They planned to cleverly arrest Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
"But not during the feast," they said. "Or the people may become stirred up." A woman poured perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethany. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
One woman came to Issa with a tightly sealed bottle containing an expensive perfume. She poured this perfume on his head while he was sitting at the table.
And my friend, when the students saw this thing, they got angry. And they said: Why this waste?
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to the poor.
Jesus perceived this. He said to them, "Why do you disturb this woman? For she has done a beautiful thing to me.
You will always have poor people among you. But I will not always be among you.
Sprinkles of perfume on my body to prepare me to be buried.
What I am about to tell you is true. What she did will be repeated about her wherever this good news is spread throughout the world. It will be repeated about her so that she will be remembered." Judas agreed to betray Jesus.
One of the twelve went to the chief priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
And he asked them, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" And they paid him thirty pieces of silver.
And since then, Judas had been watching for an opportune time to hand Jesus over to them. The Lord's Supper
That was the first day of Unleavened Bread. So the disciples came to Jesus, saying to Him, "Where do You want us to prepare for You to eat the Passover?"
He answered, "Go into the city to a certain man and say to him, 'The Teacher says: My time is near, and I will keep the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
And so, the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them to do. They prepared the Passover meal.
And when evening came, Jesus was sitting at the table with the twelve apostles.
And as they were eating, he said, "What I am about to tell you is true. One of you will betray me to my enemies."
The disciples were very sorrowful. And they began to say to him one after another: "Is it I, Lord?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dipped his hand with me in the bowl, he is the one who will betray me."
But the Son of Man will go just as it is written about him. But woe to that man by whom the Son of Man is betrayed! It would have been better for him if he had not been born!».
Judas was the one who was going to betray him. He said, "I am not him, am I, Teacher?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is you."
And as they were eating, Jesus took bread. He thanked and broke it. He gave it to His disciples and said to them: "Take this and eat it. This is My body."
Then he took the cup, blessed God, and handed it to them, saying, "All of you drink from it."
This is my blood which symbolizes the new covenant. It is shed for the forgiveness of the sins of many.
This is what I am saying to you: from now on, I will not drink wine with you again until the day I drink it with you in my Father's kingdom.
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus said that Peter would fail.
Jesus said to them, "All of you will desert me tonight, because what is written about me must be fulfilled: 'The Lord said to me, "I will strike down the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered."' (Zechariah 13:7)"
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee.'
Peter answered, "Even if they all desert you, I will never do so."
Jesus answered: "What I am about to tell you is the truth. This very night, before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times."
But Peter said, "I must die with you. I will never say that I don't know you." All the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus' Prayer in Gethsemane
And after this, Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemani. And he said to them:
And he took with him Peter and the sons of Zebedee. And he began to feel sorrow and distress.
Then He said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful, even to death. Stay here and watch with Me."
He walked a little further. And then he fell face down on the ground. He prayed and said: 'Abba, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. But let it be as you will, not as I will.'
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. He said to Peter, "Could you not watch with me for one hour?"
Watch and pray. This way you will not fall into sin when they tempt you. The spirit is ready. But the flesh is weak.
Jesus walked a little way. He prayed, saying, "My Father, is it possible for this cup to be taken away? But if I must drink it, let Your will be done."
He came back later. He found them sleeping again. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
So he left them and went away again. For the third time, he prayed the same prayer.
Then he came back to the disciples and said to them, "Are you still sleeping and taking your rest? Behold, the hour is near, and the Son of Man will be betrayed into the hands of sinners."
Wake up! Come on, let's go! Here comes the one who will hand me over to them. Jesus is being arrested.
And while Jesus was still speaking, Judas, one of the twelve, arrived with a large crowd armed with swords and clubs. The chief priests and elders of the people were the ones who had sent them.
Judas, who intended to betray Jesus, had agreed with them on a signal. He said, "The man I will kiss is the one; seize him."
Immediately Judas went to Jesus and said, "Greetings, Rabbi!" And he kissed him.
Jesus answered, "My friend, do as you have come to do." Then the men approached, seized Jesus, and arrested him.
At this very moment, one of Jesus' disciples drew his sword and laid his hand upon it. He drew it and struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back into its place. For all those who take up the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask Dad for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But how will the prophecies mentioned in the holy books be fulfilled then? They say that this must happen in this way.
At that time Jesus spoke to the crowd, saying, "Have I come out to you with a army and weapons?" And he asked them, "Was it necessary for you to come out with swords and clubs to seize me? For I was with you every day in the temple, teaching, and you did not seize me."
But all this happened so that the prophecies of the prophets would be fulfilled." Then all the disciples left him and fled. Jesus is taken to the Sanhedrin Council
And those who had arrested Jesus brought him to Caiaphas, the chief priest. Now the teachers of the law and the elders had assembled there.
Peter was following Jesus at a distance, not too far. He went straight to the courtyard of the high priest. He entered and sat down with the guards to see what would happen.
The high priests and all the members of the Sanhedrin council were searching for something they could use against Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
But they found no evidence, although many false witnesses came forward to give testimony. Finally, two other witnesses came forward to give their testimony.
They said, "This man said, 'I am able to destroy the temple of God, and to rebuild it in three days.'"
And after that, the chief priest stood up and asked Jesus: "Aren't you going to answer? What are these charges that these men are bringing against you?"
But Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God to tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
"Yes, it is as you say," Jesus answered. "But I tell all of you: In the days ahead, you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the Mighty One. You will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
And after that, that high priest tore his clothes. And he said: That one said something very bad about God! Why do we need other witnesses? I heard him say those bad words.
"What are you saying?" "He must die!" he replied.
Then they spat in his face. And they hit him with their fists. And others slapped him.
They said, "Prophecy to us, O Christ, who is he that smote thee?" Peter says that he does not know Jesus.
Peter was sitting in the courtyard; and a maid came to him, and said, "You also were with Jesus the Galilean."
But before them all, Peter said he was not. "I don't know what you are talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the gate that led to the courtyard. And there another woman saw him. And she said to the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
He said again that it wasn't so. He swore, "I don't know this man!"
Soon afterward, those who stood there went to Peter and said to him, "Surely you are one of them, for your accent betrays you."
And after that, Peter began to curse himself and swear to them, saying, "I do not know this man." And at that moment, the rooster crowed.
Peter returned and remembered what Jesus had said to him. Jesus had said, "A rooster will crow, and before it crows, you will deny me three times and tell me that you do not know me." And Peter went out to the courtyard and collapsed, weeping.
Judas hangs himself.
It was very early in the morning. And all the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled to take Jesus to death.
They bound him and led him away. Then they handed him over to Pilate, who was the governor.
Judas, the one who handed Jesus over, saw that he had been condemned to death. He felt great shame and sorrow for what he had done. He went back to the chief priests and elders and threw the 30 pieces of silver at them.
"I have sinned," he said. "I have handed over an innocent man." "What do we care?" they answered. "That's your problem."
And so, Judas threw the money into the temple and left. Then he went and hanged himself.
The senior priests collected the money. And they said: "It is illegal to put this money in the temple treasury. This is blood money. This money was paid for the death of one man."
They decided to use the money to buy a plot of land to bury the pottery. People from other countries were also going to be buried there.
This is the reason it has been called the Field of Blood until this day.
And so the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. He said: "Take thirty pieces of silver, the price at which the sons of Israel valued him."
They used the money to buy the potter's field, as the Lord commanded me. (Zechariah 11:12,13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9). Then they brought Jesus from Caiaphas to the Roman governor's headquarters.
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the chief priests and elders accused him, he made no answer.
And after that, they questioned Pilate: "Do you not hear the accusations they bring against you?"
But Jesus answered nothing, not even to a single charge. The governor was exceedingly amazed.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people could choose the one they wanted.
At that time they had a notorious prisoner called Barabbas.
When the crowd had gathered, Pilate asked them, "Whom do you want me to release for you? Barabbas? Or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were feeling jealous. He knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message saying, "Have nothing to do with that righteous man, for I have suffered much because of him today in a dream."
But the chief priests and elders persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas to be released and Jesus to be executed.
"Which of the two do you want me to release for you?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
So what shall I do with Jesus called Christ? asked Pilate. And they all answered: Crucify him.
"Why? What evil has he done?" asked Pilate. But they cried out all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was getting nowhere. On the contrary, the crowd was growing hostile. So he took a bowl of water and washed his hands in full view of them all. "I am innocent of this man's blood," he declared. "It is your responsibility."
All the people answered: "We, my children and I, will take responsibility for his death!"
Pilate released Barabbas, but ordered Jesus to be flogged, and then handed him over to be crucified on the cross. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorian. And the rest of the soldiers gathered around him.
They stripped him and dressed him in a purple robe.
And after they gathered thorns and made a crown from them. And they put it on his head. And they put a staff in his right hand. And after they fell on their knees before him and began to laugh at him. And they told him:
They spat on him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
After they mocked him, they took off his robe. They dressed him in his own clothes. Then they took him to crucify him. They crucified Jesus on the cross.
And when they went out of the city, they encountered a man of القيروان, named Simeon. And they compelled him to carry the cross.
And they came to the place that I named Golgotha. And the word Golgotha means the place of the head.
And there they mixed the wine with bitter herbs, and they offered it to him for Jesus to drink. But when he tasted it, he refused to drink it.
And when they crucified him, they divided his garments among them, casting lots.
They sat there and kept watching him.
They placed the accusation written against him on his head. It was written: "This is Jesus, the King of the Jews."
Two robbers were crucified with him, one on his right and the other on his left.
The people who passed by him started screaming at Jesus and laughing at him. They shook their heads.
And they said: «Are you going to destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days? Save yourself! If you are the Son of God, come down from the cross!».
Even the chief priests, the teachers of the law and the elders mocked him.
And they said, "He saved others, but he cannot save himself! He is the king of Israel! Let him come down now from the cross, and we will believe in him."
He believes in God. Let God save him now if he wants him. It is he who said, "I am the Son of God."
In the same way, the thieves who were crucified with Jesus also mocked him. The death of Jesus.
From noon until three o'clock, the entire earth was covered in darkness.
Jesus called out with a loud voice, saying, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" This means, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
And some of those standing there heard Jesus crying out and said, "Truly he is calling for Elijah."
Immediately, one of them ran to a couch, took a pillow, filled it with a mixture of diluted vinegar and wine, placed it on a stick, and offered it to Jesus to drink.
And the rest said: "Leave him alone. Let's see if Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
At that moment, the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom. The earth shook, and the rocks split apart.
The tombs were opened. And many bodies of the saints who were sleeping arose and came back to life.
They came out of the tombs. And after Jesus came back to life, they went to the holy city. And there they appeared to many people.
And the Roman centurion and the guards who were guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened. And they were filled with great fear. And they said, "Truly, this man was the Son of God!"
There was a large number of women watching what was happening from a short distance away. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was one of them. There was also Mary, the mother of James and Joseph. There was also the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Jesus was buried.
And when evening came, a rich man came from the city of Arimathea, named Joseph, who had become a follower of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for the body of Jesus. Pilate gave orders for it to be given to him.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth.
And he placed it in his new grave, which he had carved out of the rock. And he rolled a large stone in front of the entrance to the grave. Then he went away.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards at the tomb
The next day was the day after the preparation day. So the chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
"Sir," they said, "we recall something that liar said when he was still alive. He claimed: 'After three days I will rise again.'"
So order the tomb to be secured until the third day, otherwise his disciples may come and steal the body, and then tell the people that Jesus has risen from the dead, and this last lie will be worse than the first.
Pilate answered, "Take some guards with you. Go and make the tomb as secure as you can."
They went and secured the tomb. They sealed the stone and placed guards to observe.
Jesus rises from the dead.
Saturday had now ended. It was dawn on the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to see the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. The Angel of the Lord descended from heaven. And the angel went to the tomb. So he rolled away the stone and sat on it.
His body was shining like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid, for I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
He's not here! He has returned as he said! Come and see the place where he was sleeping.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples: 'Behold, he has risen from the dead, and he will go before you to Galilee; there you will see him.' This is what I told you while I was still with you.
The two women returned, hurrying from the tomb, and they were afraid, and at the same time filled with joy. And they ran to tell the disciples.
And suddenly Jesus appeared to them and said, "Peace be with you!" They approached Him and held His feet and worshiped Him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The Guard's Report to the Chief Priests
And while the women were on their way, some guards entered the city. And they told the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the high priests met with the elders, they devised a plan. They gave the soldiers a large sum of money.
They told the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the governor hears about this report, we will bribe him. This will save you from trouble.
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story has been spread among Jews to this day. The last command that Jesus gave to his disciples.
And after that, the 11 disciples went to Galilee. And they went to the mountain to which Jesus had told them to go.
But when they saw him, they prostrated themselves to him, but some of them were suspicious.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me.
Therefore, you must go and make disciples of all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And be certain that I am always with you, to the bitter end."
John the Baptist paves the way.
This is the beginning of the good news about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote: "I will send my messenger ahead of you, who will prepare your way." (Malachi 3:1)
A messenger is calling in the desert: "Prepare the way for the Lord, make his paths straight." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He was baptizing people in the desert. He also preached to people about the necessity of being baptized and repenting of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the people of the countryside of Judea went out to him, and all the people of Jerusalem went out to him. When they admitted that they had sinned, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John was wearing clothes made of camel hair. He was wearing a leather belt around his waist. He was eating locusts and wild honey.
This is what John was proclaiming: "One is coming after me who is mightier than I. I am not worthy to stoop down and untie the thong of His sandals."
I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The baptism of Jesus and his temptation.
At that time, Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee. And John the Baptist baptized him in the River Jordan.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. And at that moment, he saw the heavens opened up. And he saw the Holy Spirit descend on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven spoke to him, saying, "You are my son, whom I love. I am very pleased with you."
And immediately the Spirit sent Jesus out into the wilderness.
He was in the desert for 40 days. And there the devil tested him. The wild animals did not harm Jesus. And the angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was arrested, Jesus went to Galilee. And he began to preach the good news of God.
He said, "The time has come, the Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!"
Jesus was once walking by the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets immediately and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little further ahead. And as he walked, he saw James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat mending their nets.
Immediately he called them. So they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, and they followed Jesus. Jesus Expels an Unclean Spirit
Jesus and those who were with him walked to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath day came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
People were astonished at his teaching, for he taught them as one having authority, and not as the scribes.
A man who was in their assembly at that moment, and who had an evil spirit that would control him, stood up and said:
Ashno bghiti 3andna, ya Yasou3 An-Naseri? Wachejiti bash t'helkna? 3aref chkon nta. Nta howa L'Qudous dial Allah!
"Be quiet!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him, screaming.
Everyone was amazed. They said to each other, "What is this? A new teaching! And with authority! He even commands evil spirits, and they obey him."
The news about Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those who were with him left the synagogue and immediately went with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was feverish. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand, helped her up, and the fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all the sick. They also brought all those possessed by demons.
All the townspeople gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from all kinds of diseases. He also cast out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus' prayer in a quiet place
It was very early in the morning, and the darkness was still over all. Jesus rose and left the house. And he went to a place where he could be alone. And there he prayed.
Simon and his companions went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out: "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to some other place. I want to go to the neighboring towns. I must preach there also. For this reason I have come."
So he traveled throughout all of Galilee, and was teaching in their synagogues. And he was casting out demons as well. Jesus Heals a Man with a Skin Disease
A man who was suffering from a skin disease came to Jesus. He fell on his knees, begging Jesus. He said, "If you want to make me 'clean,' you can."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
The illness left him immediately. He has recovered.
Jesus immediately sent him away. He gave the man a strong warning.
He said, "Don't tell anyone about this. Go and present yourself to the priest. Offer the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be proof for the priest and for the people that you are 'pure.'"
But the man went out and began to talk at once. And he spread the news among everyone. So Jesus was no longer able to enter the city openly. And he remained outside in secluded places. But the people kept coming to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who was not able to walk.
After a few days, Jesus returned to Capernaum. And the people heard that he had come back to his house.
A large crowd of people gathered to the point that there was no more room. There wasn't even room outside the door. And Jesus preached the word to them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't get him close enough to Jesus because of the crowd. So they dug an opening through the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on his stretcher right down through the hole.
Isa saw their faith. And he said to the man: My son, your sins are forgiven you.
Some law professors were sitting there. They were thinking,
Why is this gentleman speaking in this manner? He is saying something very bad! Only God is capable of forgiving sins!
And Jesus immediately knew their thoughts, and said to them, "Why do you think this?
Is it easy to tell this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to tell him, "Get up, pick up your mat, and go"?
I tell you, the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then Jesus said to the man who could not walk:
"I'm telling you," he said, "get up. Take your mat and go home."
The man stood up and took his mat. Then he walked away while everyone was watching him. And all the people were amazed. And they praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Jesus went out again beside the Sea of Galilee. A great multitude came to him. And he began to teach them.
And as Jesus was walking, he saw Levi the son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting at the tax collector's booth. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Levi got up and followed him.
After that, Jesus was having dinner at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that was following Jesus.
There were some Pharisee teachers of the law. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard these words, and he said to them, "The healthy do not need a doctor, but the sick do. I did not come to call the righteous to repentance, but the sinners." A question was posed to Jesus about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. Some people came to Jesus. And they said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees fast. But your disciples do not fast. Why do they not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as the bridegroom is with them.
But a time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will not eat anything.
People do not sew a new patch of cloth onto old clothes. If they did this, the new patch would be dragged by the old clothes. This would cause the hole to become even larger.
They don't put new wine into old wineskins. Otherwise, the wine will burst the skins, and both the wine and the skins will be ruined. No, people put new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath day, Jesus was walking through wheat fields with his disciples. The disciples began to pick some of the wheat heads.
The Pharisees said to Jesus: "Look! This man is doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath. For the Son of Man is Lord
He answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate consecrated bread. Only priests were allowed to eat it. David even gave some of it to his men as well.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath.
Therefore, the Son of Man is also Lord of the Sabbath."
Healing on Saturday.
And Jesus went again into the synagogue. And there was a man whose hand was withered and distorted.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and crooked hand. He said to him, "Stand up in front of everyone."
And after that, Jesus asked them: "What does the law say that we must do on the Sabbath day? Must we do good? Or must we do evil? Must we save life? Or must we kill?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them in anger, deeply distressed because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and his hand was restored, just like the new hand.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds follow Jesus.
Jesus walked with his disciples to the Sea of Galilee. A large crowd from Galilee followed them.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing, and many people came to him. They came from Judea and Jerusalem and Idumea. They came from the region east of the Jordan River. And they came from the area around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This way he would avoid being surrounded by people.
Jesus had healed many people. Therefore, the sick were pushing to touch him.
And the people who had spirits of uncleanness, fell down before Him. And there arose a great cry, saying, "This is the son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell anyone who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up into the mountain, and called unto him certain people, and they came unto him.
And he chose 12 of them and called them apostles. And from then on, they were with him. And he also sent them out to preach.
They would have had the power to cast out demons.
And Jesus chose the twelve disciples. Simon was one of them. And Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus named them Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus. And there was Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He was the one who would later betray Jesus to his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. Once again, a crowd gathered. The crowd was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about this, so they came to take him under their care. They said, "He is out of his mind."
There were some Pharisees who came down from Jerusalem and said, 'By Beelzebul, the prince of demons, he is driving out demons!'
Jesus summoned them and spoke to them in parables, saying, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom is fighting against itself, it will not be able to stand.
If a family is divided, it cannot stand.
